    "title": "pandas.Series.str.rfind — pandas 3.1.0.dev0 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Series.str.rfind#\n\n- \nSeries.str.rfind(*sub* ,*start=0* ,*end=None* )[source]#\n- Return highest indexes in each strings in the Series/Index. Each of returned indexes corresponds to the position where the substring is fully contained between [start:end]. Return -1 on failure. Equivalent to standard `str.rfind()` .\n  - Parameters:\n    - **sub** str\n    - Substring being searched.\n    - **start** int\n    - Left edge index.\n    - **end** int\n    - Right edge index.\n  - Returns:\n    - Series or Index of int.\n    - A Series (if the input is a Series) or an Index (if the input is an Index) of the highest indexes corresponding to the positions where the substring is found in each string of the input.\n See also \n  - `find`\n  - Return lowest indexes in each strings.\n Examples For Series.str.find: >>> ser = pd.Series([\"_cow_\", \"duck_\", \"do_v_e\"]) >>> ser.str.find(\"_\") 0 0 1 4 2 2 dtype: int64 For Series.str.rfind: >>> ser = pd.Series([\"_cow_\", \"duck_\", \"do_v_e\"]) >>> ser.str.rfind(\"_\") 0 4 1 4 2 4 dtype: int64",
    "markdown_length": 1023,
